{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel journeyed from Be'erot Benei Ya'akan to Moserah</b> — what is the relevance of this here? Furthermore, did they really journey from Be'erot Benei Ya'akan to Moserah? Did they not come from Moserah to Benei Ya'akan, as it is stated (Numbers 33:31), \"And they journeyed from Moserot and encamped at Benei Ya'akan\"? And furthermore, <b>there Aaron died</b> — but did he not die at Hor HaHar? Go and count, and you will find eight stations from Moserot to Hor HaHar. Rather, this too is among the reproofs: \"This too you did\" — when Aaron died at Hor HaHar at the end of forty years and the clouds of glory departed, you feared battle against the king of Arad, and you resolved to turn back toward Egypt. You turned backward eight stations until Benei Ya'akan, and from there to Moserah. There the children of Levi fought against you and killed some of you, and you some of them, until they turned you back to the way. From there you returned to Gudgodah, which is Hor HaGidgad, and from Gudgodah to Moserah. And in Moserah you made a great mourning over the death of Aaron, since it had caused all this for you, and it seemed to you as though he had died there. And Moses attached this reproof to the breaking of the tablets, to say that the death of the righteous before the Holy One, blessed be He, is as difficult as the day the tablets were broken, and to inform you that what they said — <b>\"Let us appoint a head\"</b> — to separate from Him, was as difficult to Him as the day they made the calf.\n\n\"At that time HaShem separated the tribe of Levi\" — this refers back to the first matter: \"At that time,\" in the first year after you came out of Egypt, you went astray with the calf, and the children of Levi did not go astray; the Place separated them from among you. And this is attached to the return at Benei Ya'akan, to say that in this matter too the children of Levi did not go astray but stood firm in their faithfulness. All of this is the language of our Rabbi Solomon, from aggadic sources (Yerushalmi Yoma 1:1).\n\nRabbi Abraham found it necessary to say by way of peshat that Be'erot Benei Ya'akan is not Benei Ya'akan, and Moserah is not Moserot; rather, Kadesh is called Be'erot Benei Ya'akan, and the wilderness of Hor HaHar is called Moserah. \"From there they journeyed to Gudgodah\" — that is not Hor HaGidgad but a general name for Tzalmonah and Punon and Ovot, and Yotvatah is the place called Be'erah (Numbers 21:16), which is the meaning of \"a land of brooks of water.\" And <b>\"And from Gudgodah to Yotvatah\"</b> attaches to <b>\"and he [Elazar] served as priest,\"</b> for there at Yotvatah Elazar served in the high priesthood and offered sacrifices. But all these are words of wind, for how is it possible that all these many places should all be new places not mentioned in the itinerary of the journeys? Furthermore, it is the way of Scripture everywhere to vary slightly when it mentions names, sometimes for a place name and sometimes for a person's name, and all the more so here, where they are identical: Moserah and Moserot are not a change and a difference, and likewise Hor HaGidgad and HaGudgodah are one name, and Yotvatah is exactly the same. Furthermore, Elazar served as priest at Hor HaHar when his father died, at the time they dressed him in the garments, for immediately he was invested with the garments, and this is the procedure for high priests even for future generations.\n\nWhat seems right in my eyes is that Hor HaHar is the greatest of mountains and extends many parasangs, and its name proves that it is called \"Hor,\" and they say <b>\"the mountain\"</b> about it because of its greatness and length. And so our Sages said (Bamidbar Rabbah 19:16) that it was a mountain upon a mountain, and the end of the mountain reached the border of the land of Edom while its head was near Cheshmonah. And in the plain that was before Hor HaHar there were cities or places called Moserot and Benei Ya'akan and Hor HaGidgad and Yotvatah and Avaronah and Etzyon Gaver and Kadesh. And these places are not far from one another, for that great camp travels in a single day about a parasang or less. They journeyed from Kadesh and encamped at Hor HaHar — they entered it at a low place that was at the foot of the mountain, and then HaShem commanded Moses (Numbers 20:25), <b>\"and bring them up to Hor HaHar,\"</b> meaning he should go up to its head, which is the beginning of the mountain. Now they went up to the top of the mountain and arrived opposite Moserot, and Aaron died there, as it is stated (ibid. v. 28), <b>\"and Aaron died there on the summit of the mountain\"</b> — and so his death was at Moserah. It is possible that these places were known by their names on the mountain itself, and the top of the mountain is called by the name opposite it in the plain, Moserot, and each one is called Moserah, so that they would say \"Moserah that is on the mountain\" and \"Moserah that is in the lowland.\" And at the place called Cheshmonah there were wells in the field; they called them Be'erot Benei Ya'akan, for the men of Benei Ya'akan would come there to water their flocks, and they [the wells] were made by them because there they found living water. From there they journeyed to Moserah, and from there they journeyed to Moserot: \"And they journeyed from Moserot and encamped at Benei Ya'akan\" — that place itself is its name. And Scripture recounts, \"From there they journeyed to Gudgodah, and from Gudgodah to Yotvatah, a land of brooks of water,\" to inform us that they stayed there for days because of the streams. And it abbreviated their itinerary, for it had already explained them, but said that from there, from Moserah, they journeyed to Gudgodah and to Yotvatah, which is entirely a land of brooks of water, for from Gudgod to Yotvatah it is all irrigated land.\n\nIt is further possible that when Aaron went up to the top of the mountain and died at the top of the mountain opposite Moserot or in Moserah itself, and was buried there, Israel returned to Moserot to weep and lament over the grave of the man of Elohim. And for thirty days their leaders would go up to the top of the mountain and eulogize the holy one of HaShem, and they would return to the camp at Moserah. And after the thirty days they journeyed from Moserah to Hor HaGidgad, and from Gudgodah to Yotvatah — for now on their return they traveled quickly. And this is very sound in resolving the verses according to their contextual arrangement. And he mentioned that the name of the place was Yotvatah because it was a good place, for there were streams of water there, while the rest of the wilderness was a land of aridity and drought where there was no water.\n\nThe reason Aaron's death is mentioned now in this place is that he said (Deuteronomy 9:20), \"And I prayed also on behalf of Aaron at that time,\" and [to show that] the punishment of extinction did not reach him, neither in his person nor in his seed, for he did not die until the fortieth year, when he was old and full of days, and his son served as priest in his stead. And it says, \"And the children of Israel journeyed,\" and does not say <b>\"and you\"</b> as throughout the pericah — for in the reproofs he speaks to them directly to rebuke them, but in this matter he recounts the journeys of the children of Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The text refers to Rashi as 'רבינו שלמה' (our Rabbi Solomon) and to Ibn Ezra as 'רבי אברהם' (Rabbi Abraham), following the Hebrew original's conventions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:48:02.250288600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}